        "title": "Metabolic and Endocrine Disease",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "**Systems Bridge:** The endocrine system is a collection of hierarchically nested negative-feedback control loops, and endocrine disease is what happens when those loops break down. The HPT axis (hypothalamus → pituitary → thyroid) is a three-level cascade with set points and error signals at each level: CRH, TSH, and T3/T4 form a proportional controller with integral windup (chronic low T4 drives sustained TSH elevation). Insulin regulation is a simpler proportional controller: glucose is the error signal, insulin release is the corrective output, glucose uptake is the plant response. Type 2 diabetes is receptor desensitization — input-output gain drops (more insulin required per unit glucose correction), the controller compensates with higher output (hyperinsulinemia), and eventually the output source (β-cell mass) degrades from chronic overload. Type 1 diabetes is actuator failure — the insulin-secreting actuator is destroyed, not the sensor or the set point. Cushing's disease is a stuck-high disturbance input (cortisol) that overwhelms the feedback loop; the high-dose dexamethasone suppression test probes whether the controller's regulatory gain is intact (pituitary adenoma suppresses; ectopic source does not). Understanding endocrine disease as control-loop failure lets you predict: primary gland failure (high set-point error signal, high compensatory hormone up the axis); secondary failure (low compensatory hormone from the pituitary; low downstream output); ectopic secretion (source outside the loop, unresponsive to feedback)."
    }
